        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T19:24:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not all of them need it, only those with ATC_REQUIRED, right?\n\nBut I think the most important thing to mention here is that all devices listed in SATC are _trusted_ to use ATS in a secure way (according to VT-d spec which says that all such devices must provide a \"Host Physical Address Isolation Boundary\", so even if such device is compromised, it is still not able to do malicious DMA requests at arbitrary addresses). Which is the reason why we assume that allowing ATS for them will not compromise pKVM security guarantees.",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "56f2e856_fce46a3d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T01:11:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason we do an ioremap for the IOMMU\u0027s MMIO and read the cap/ecap from the hardware is that, the drhd-\u003eiommu-\u003ecap/ecap is already modified by pkvm_update_iommu_virtual_caps() at this point, e.g. the nested ecap is removed by this function. I guess with this change, even dmar2 also has the warning \"nested translation not supported\" below, which is expected only for dmar0 and dmar1.\n\nIf this is true, then it is better to make the pkvm hypervisor to read the cap/ecap from the hardware rather than getting from the info-\u003ecap/ecap (the change in hyp/iommu.c)",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f7f55095_c4f5557f",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 4168684
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T01:59:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the ecap/ecap is read from the HW again, the ECAP.DT needs to be updated for iommu* based on the SATC parsed entries. Those SATC entries \u0027dmar_satc_units\u0027 are static to the driver hence can\u0027t be accessed in the PKVM hypervisor. Thus copying the cap/ecap from the host here.\nTo avoid the warning for dmar2, the hypervisor can still read from HW and check for NEST and but the copy to pkvm_iommu_inf-\u003ecap/ecap should be from the host iommu ecap/ecap. Does that sound okay?",
      "parentUuid": "56f2e856_fce46a3d",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "62677363_2d8dbcfd",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T02:21:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I feel that, the key point implemented by this patch is to set the DT bit in IOMMU\u0027s ecap if the SATC has ATC_REQUIRED flag. And then in the hyp/iommu.c the device iotlb enabling is based on whether the DT bit is set or not. That is why you don\u0027t want to read from hardware directly, as the value from the hardware is not based on the SATC parsing result.\n\nHowever, with this patch, for example, if an IOMMU contains two devices, device A is described by the SATC, but the device B is not. Then seems this patch will set the DT bit for this IOMMU. And the pkvm hypervisor will enable device iotlb for both device A and device B, but in fact, we only want the device A to have device iotlb as device B is not described in the SATC.",
      "parentUuid": "f7f55095_c4f5557f",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4c0181d9_1a2f5ce0",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 4168684
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T04:31:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As per the PTL design spec, Gfx is mapped to one remapping HW unit, Non-Gfx (NPU, IPU, PCMS) mapped to another, all other dev to the last remapping unit. And Intel supports DevTLB in all Gfx \u0026 non-Gfx devices (IOMMU0 \u0026 IOMMU1), thus guarantees security in those 4 integrated devices in the SOC. So even though if SATC lists just one device from a group, enabling devtlb for all the devices should be safe.",
      "parentUuid": "62677363_2d8dbcfd",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f11bb16_a43fb4ac",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T05:35:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding the warning for dmar2 \"nested translation not supported\", this will impact the pkvm hypervisor to use the nested translation as the pkvm hypervisor cannot see this cap now. This should be fixed.",
      "parentUuid": "4c0181d9_1a2f5ce0",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dab3ba9c_3edd19a3",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 199,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-04T19:24:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e However, with this patch, for example, if an IOMMU contains two devices, device A is described by the SATC, but the device B is not. Then seems this patch will set the DT bit for this IOMMU. And the pkvm hypervisor will enable device iotlb for both device A and device B, but in fact, we only want the device A to have device iotlb as device B is not described in the SATC.\n\nI think that\u0027s the key issue. We should allow ATS per device, not per IOMMU. For instance, on PTL, according to the latest ACPI reference in coreboot (https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/coreboot/+/refs/heads/main/src/soc/intel/pantherlake/acpi.c#248), there may be up to 5 devices behind the same Dmar2 IOMMU: NPU, IPU, IAA, telemetry unit and thermal unit (DPTF), and only three of them (NPU, IPU, IAA) are listed in SATC. So IIUC only three of them actually use ATS and are trusted to use it securely, the other two don\u0027t.\n\n(For instance, according to Intel, DPTF doesn\u0027t even do any DMA, so it is pointless to allow ATS for it. I don\u0027t know about the telemetry unit. If it can do DMA, and at the same time doesn\u0027t have a trusted boundary for ATS like the GPU, NPU and IPU do, it means that by allowing ATS for it we would introduce a security hole.)\n\nSo I think the approach of this patch is wrong.\n\nCan\u0027t we just add a new field to struct pkvm_ptdev, e.g. `devtlb_allowed`, instead of hacking the virtual caps?\n\nI was imagining we could implement this in a simple way: when initially before deprivilege in check_and_init_iommu() we scan DRHDs and save the information about IOMMUs in pkvm-\u003eiommu_infos, we can additionally scan `dmar_satc_units` and save the information about SATC devices as well. (For that we might need to export dmar_satc_units in dmar.h and maybe add for_each_satc_unit(), but that\u0027s a detail.) And then later when we register devices in iommu_add_ptdev() -\u003e pkvm_alloc_ptdev(), we use this information to set ptdev-\u003edevtlb_allowed accordingly.",
      "parentUuid": "7f11bb16_a43fb4ac",
      "revId": "ffd1c18811a50d7d96a4b9198f4d2a44265d85f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
